1
00:00:00,020 --> 00:00:01,900
Why does your refrigerator have to heat up

2
00:00:01,920 --> 00:00:04,340
to keep your food cold?

3
00:00:04,360 --> 00:00:05,420
It sounds backwards.

4
00:00:05,440 --> 00:00:07,180
But put your hand on the side of a running

5
00:00:07,200 --> 00:00:08,900
fridge, or near the grille at the bottom,

6
00:00:08,920 --> 00:00:10,630
and you'll often feel warmth.

7
00:00:10,800 --> 00:00:12,620
In a hot room, the sides of some models

8
00:00:12,640 --> 00:00:14,770
get hotter than fifty degrees Celsius.

9
00:00:15,040 --> 00:00:16,300
That's about a hundred and twenty

10
00:00:16,320 --> 00:00:17,030
Fahrenheit.

11
00:00:17,240 --> 00:00:18,690
That heat isn't a defect.

12
00:00:18,760 --> 00:00:19,890
It's the whole trick.

13
00:00:20,040 --> 00:00:22,080
And tucked away inside, right next to the

14
00:00:22,100 --> 00:00:23,980
coldest part of the machine, there's a

15
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:24,910
second heater.

16
00:00:25,100 --> 00:00:26,910
One that switches on on purpose.

17
00:00:30,860 --> 00:00:32,520
Let's start with the way we talk about

18
00:00:32,540 --> 00:00:33,130
cold.

19
00:00:33,260 --> 00:00:35,530
We talk about cold as if it were a thing.

20
00:00:35,740 --> 00:00:36,620
Let the cold out.

21
00:00:36,640 --> 00:00:37,620
Keep the cold in.

22
00:00:37,640 --> 00:00:39,070
But there's no such substance.

23
00:00:39,280 --> 00:00:40,660
There is no cold to add.

24
00:00:40,680 --> 00:00:42,660
What exists is heat: energy that flows

25
00:00:42,680 --> 00:00:44,570
because of a difference in temperature.

26
00:00:44,760 --> 00:00:46,950
And on its own, heat only flows one way.

27
00:00:47,060 --> 00:00:48,190
From hotter to colder.

28
00:00:48,300 --> 00:00:49,920
A hot mug warms your hands.

29
00:00:49,940 --> 00:00:52,000
A cold drink never chills them by pushing

30
00:00:52,020 --> 00:00:52,910
cold in.

31
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:55,060
Your hands are simply losing heat to it.

32
00:00:55,080 --> 00:00:57,110
So a refrigerator doesn't make cold.

33
00:00:57,980 --> 00:00:59,090
It removes heat.

34
00:00:59,240 --> 00:01:00,940
Your milk is cold because heat has been

35
00:01:00,960 --> 00:01:01,780
taken out of it.

36
00:01:01,800 --> 00:01:03,050
Here's the problem.

37
00:01:03,240 --> 00:01:04,940
Your kitchen is warmer than the inside of

38
00:01:04,960 --> 00:01:05,690
your fridge.

39
00:01:05,900 --> 00:01:07,900
Getting heat out of the cold box and into

40
00:01:07,920 --> 00:01:09,740
the warm room means moving it in the one

41
00:01:09,760 --> 00:01:11,790
direction it will never go by itself.

42
00:01:12,200 --> 00:01:13,970
Physicists have a name for this rule.

43
00:01:14,140 --> 00:01:15,860
It's the Clausius statement of the second

44
00:01:15,880 --> 00:01:17,290
law of thermodynamics.

45
00:01:17,600 --> 00:01:19,720
Moving heat from a cold place to a warmer

46
00:01:19,740 --> 00:01:20,890
one takes work.

47
00:01:21,040 --> 00:01:23,170
So your fridge works like a heat pump.

48
00:01:23,980 --> 00:01:26,400
It spends electricity to push heat uphill,

49
00:01:26,420 --> 00:01:29,370
out of your food and into your kitchen.

50
00:01:29,860 --> 00:01:32,320
To pull that off, it uses one fluid

51
00:01:32,340 --> 00:01:34,000
travelling around and around a sealed

52
00:01:34,020 --> 00:01:34,570
loop.

53
00:01:34,780 --> 00:01:35,720
The refrigerant.

54
00:01:35,740 --> 00:01:37,760
Follow one drop of it all the way round,

55
00:01:37,780 --> 00:01:39,390
and the whole machine makes sense.

56
00:01:39,700 --> 00:01:41,490
The trick it relies on is boiling.

57
00:01:41,780 --> 00:01:43,500
A liquid's boiling point depends on

58
00:01:43,520 --> 00:01:44,150
pressure.

59
00:01:44,480 --> 00:01:46,260
Lower the pressure, and it boils at a

60
00:01:46,280 --> 00:01:47,270
lower temperature.

61
00:01:47,740 --> 00:01:49,540
Raise the pressure, and the boiling point

62
00:01:49,560 --> 00:01:50,190
climbs.

63
00:01:51,000 --> 00:01:52,700
Household refrigerants boil far below

64
00:01:52,720 --> 00:01:55,570
freezing, even at ordinary air pressure.

65
00:01:56,660 --> 00:01:58,560
Isobutane, one of the common ones, boils

66
00:01:58,580 --> 00:02:00,650
at about eleven degrees Fahrenheit.

67
00:02:00,940 --> 00:02:01,830
Now, the loop.

68
00:02:02,060 --> 00:02:04,180
Stop one is the evaporator, a coil inside

69
00:02:04,200 --> 00:02:04,890
the fridge.

70
00:02:05,180 --> 00:02:06,600
Here the refrigerant is kept at low

71
00:02:06,620 --> 00:02:08,800
pressure, so it boils, and boiling soaks

72
00:02:08,820 --> 00:02:09,470
up heat.

73
00:02:09,680 --> 00:02:11,550
That heat comes from inside the fridge.

74
00:02:11,760 --> 00:02:13,640
From the air, the shelves, last night's

75
00:02:13,660 --> 00:02:15,420
leftovers, and the warmth that keeps

76
00:02:15,440 --> 00:02:17,490
creeping in through the walls and door.

77
00:02:17,700 --> 00:02:19,290
This is where it all ends up.

78
00:02:19,600 --> 00:02:21,030
Stop two is the compressor.

79
00:02:21,600 --> 00:02:24,060
The cold, low pressure vapor flows into

80
00:02:24,080 --> 00:02:26,240
it, and the compressor squeezes it into a

81
00:02:26,260 --> 00:02:28,290
high pressure vapor that is much hotter.

82
00:02:28,600 --> 00:02:29,900
Hot enough to be well above room

83
00:02:29,920 --> 00:02:30,750
temperature.

84
00:02:31,080 --> 00:02:32,740
This is where the electricity does its

85
00:02:32,760 --> 00:02:33,610
main job.

86
00:02:33,840 --> 00:02:35,530
The compressor is doing the work.

87
00:02:35,920 --> 00:02:37,450
Stop three is the condenser.

88
00:02:37,800 --> 00:02:39,940
Depending on the design, that's a coil on

89
00:02:39,960 --> 00:02:42,120
the back, tubing hidden inside the side

90
00:02:42,140 --> 00:02:44,580
walls, or a coil in a compartment at the

91
00:02:44,600 --> 00:02:47,110
bottom with a fan blowing across it.

92
00:02:47,800 --> 00:02:49,600
The vapor arriving here is hotter than

93
00:02:49,620 --> 00:02:51,740
your kitchen, so heat flows out of it into

94
00:02:51,760 --> 00:02:52,450
the room.

95
00:02:52,660 --> 00:02:53,770
The easy direction.

96
00:02:53,960 --> 00:02:56,300
As it loses heat, the vapor condenses back

97
00:02:56,320 --> 00:02:58,550
into a liquid, still under high pressure.

98
00:02:58,940 --> 00:03:00,770
Stop four is the expansion device.

99
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:02,760
In a typical household fridge it's a

100
00:03:02,780 --> 00:03:05,140
capillary tube, a long, very thin tube

101
00:03:05,160 --> 00:03:06,530
that restricts the flow.

102
00:03:06,700 --> 00:03:08,620
It holds back the high pressure side from

103
00:03:08,640 --> 00:03:09,990
the low pressure side.

104
00:03:10,200 --> 00:03:12,360
Past it, the pressure drops, the boiling

105
00:03:12,380 --> 00:03:14,540
point drops with it, and the refrigerant

106
00:03:14,560 --> 00:03:15,990
starts to boil again.

107
00:03:16,520 --> 00:03:19,020
In many fridges this tube is soldered to

108
00:03:19,040 --> 00:03:21,100
the line carrying cold vapor back to the

109
00:03:21,120 --> 00:03:23,400
compressor, so the warm liquid hands some

110
00:03:23,420 --> 00:03:25,270
of its heat to that vapor on the way.

111
00:03:25,480 --> 00:03:27,060
And then the refrigerant is back in the

112
00:03:27,080 --> 00:03:29,410
evaporator, and the loop starts again.

113
00:03:29,820 --> 00:03:31,030
Now do the accounting.

114
00:03:31,300 --> 00:03:32,800
Energy is conserved.

115
00:03:32,820 --> 00:03:34,680
So the heat the fridge dumps into your

116
00:03:34,700 --> 00:03:37,000
kitchen equals all the heat it pulled out

117
00:03:37,020 --> 00:03:39,600
of the inside, plus the electrical energy

118
00:03:39,620 --> 00:03:41,170
it used to do it.

119
00:03:41,760 --> 00:03:44,200
On a tough standard test, a household

120
00:03:44,220 --> 00:03:47,200
compressor moves roughly one point four to

121
00:03:47,220 --> 00:03:49,460
two units of heat for every unit of

122
00:03:49,480 --> 00:03:51,050
electricity it uses.

123
00:03:51,980 --> 00:03:54,120
So here's the missing context to the

124
00:03:54,140 --> 00:03:54,770
title.

125
00:03:55,220 --> 00:03:56,710
Heat only flows downhill.

126
00:03:56,960 --> 00:03:58,460
For heat to leave the condenser on its

127
00:03:58,480 --> 00:04:00,480
own, the refrigerant inside has to be

128
00:04:00,500 --> 00:04:02,110
hotter than the air in your kitchen.

129
00:04:02,360 --> 00:04:04,380
That's the compressor's whole job: push it

130
00:04:04,400 --> 00:04:05,870
well above room temperature.

131
00:04:06,060 --> 00:04:07,340
A condenser that stayed at room

132
00:04:07,360 --> 00:04:09,170
temperature couldn't get rid of anything.

133
00:04:09,600 --> 00:04:11,700
Dust, lint and pet hair on the condenser

134
00:04:11,720 --> 00:04:13,720
block the airflow and make it run hotter,

135
00:04:13,740 --> 00:04:16,080
so the compressor has to work harder and

136
00:04:16,100 --> 00:04:16,710
longer.

137
00:04:17,120 --> 00:04:18,800
Hotpoint says to clean the coils at least

138
00:04:18,820 --> 00:04:19,850
once a year.

139
00:04:20,720 --> 00:04:22,700
Some models don't need routine cleaning at

140
00:04:22,720 --> 00:04:23,330
all.

141
00:04:23,660 --> 00:04:24,790
Space matters too.

142
00:04:25,360 --> 00:04:27,260
Manufacturers specify air gaps, and they

143
00:04:27,280 --> 00:04:28,210
vary a lot.

144
00:04:28,520 --> 00:04:30,760
One G E side by side asks for just an

145
00:04:30,780 --> 00:04:32,150
eighth of an inch at the sides.

146
00:04:32,860 --> 00:04:34,210
Samsung recommends at least five

147
00:04:34,300 --> 00:04:36,060
centimetres, about two inches, at the

148
00:04:36,080 --> 00:04:37,070
sides and back.

149
00:04:37,220 --> 00:04:39,080
And putting a fridge next to an oven, a

150
00:04:39,100 --> 00:04:41,500
dishwasher, a heating vent, or in direct

151
00:04:41,520 --> 00:04:43,440
sunlight makes it harder for it to shed

152
00:04:43,460 --> 00:04:44,070
heat.

153
00:04:44,540 --> 00:04:45,640
Energy guides and installation

154
00:04:45,660 --> 00:04:47,890
instructions say to avoid it.

155
00:04:48,440 --> 00:04:49,950
Now for that second heater.

156
00:04:50,220 --> 00:04:52,130
The evaporator stays below freezing.

157
00:04:52,420 --> 00:04:53,920
And the air inside your fridge carries

158
00:04:53,940 --> 00:04:56,360
moisture, from your food, and from warm,

159
00:04:56,380 --> 00:04:58,140
humid kitchen air that rushes in every

160
00:04:58,160 --> 00:04:59,450
time you open the door.

161
00:04:59,700 --> 00:05:01,540
That moisture condenses on the cold coil

162
00:05:01,560 --> 00:05:02,370
and freezes.

163
00:05:03,160 --> 00:05:03,640
Frost.

164
00:05:03,660 --> 00:05:05,290
Frost conducts heat very poorly.

165
00:05:05,480 --> 00:05:07,040
A layer of it insulates the coil and

166
00:05:07,060 --> 00:05:09,140
chokes the airflow, so the fridge cools

167
00:05:09,160 --> 00:05:10,770
worse and wastes energy.

168
00:05:11,140 --> 00:05:12,940
A frost free fridge solves it with an

169
00:05:12,960 --> 00:05:15,560
electric heater, mounted on or just below

170
00:05:15,580 --> 00:05:16,730
the evaporator.

171
00:05:17,460 --> 00:05:19,600
Often a metal rod, or a heating element

172
00:05:19,620 --> 00:05:21,210
inside a glass tube.

173
00:05:21,340 --> 00:05:23,260
While it's on, it can draw about three

174
00:05:23,280 --> 00:05:25,290
times as much power as the compressor.

175
00:05:25,540 --> 00:05:27,780
In the two service manuals we checked, the

176
00:05:27,800 --> 00:05:29,380
heater drew about four hundred and fifty

177
00:05:29,400 --> 00:05:31,390
to four hundred and seventy watts.

178
00:05:31,640 --> 00:05:33,224
The compressor, roughly a hundred and

179
00:05:33,244 --> 00:05:35,300
twenty to a hundred and sixty.

180
00:05:35,320 --> 00:05:37,380
But the heater only runs for minutes at a

181
00:05:37,400 --> 00:05:38,010
time.

182
00:05:38,300 --> 00:05:39,650
So when does it switch on?

183
00:05:39,720 --> 00:05:41,820
It goes by how long the compressor has

184
00:05:41,840 --> 00:05:42,770
been running.

185
00:05:43,660 --> 00:05:45,560
Older timers start a defrost about every

186
00:05:45,580 --> 00:05:46,830
eight hours of running.

187
00:05:47,200 --> 00:05:49,650
Newer controls adapt: keep the doors shut,

188
00:05:49,700 --> 00:05:51,480
and some models wait a hundred hours or

189
00:05:51,500 --> 00:05:52,050
more.

190
00:05:52,120 --> 00:05:53,190
Then there's the water.

191
00:05:53,380 --> 00:05:55,480
In many fridges, the melted frost drains

192
00:05:55,500 --> 00:05:57,340
through a tube to a shallow pan down near

193
00:05:57,360 --> 00:05:58,250
the condenser.

194
00:05:58,560 --> 00:06:00,580
There, the condenser's warmth, and in many

195
00:06:00,600 --> 00:06:02,940
models its fan, evaporate the water back

196
00:06:02,960 --> 00:06:03,720
into your kitchen.

197
00:06:03,740 --> 00:06:06,660
The fridge uses its own waste heat to get

198
00:06:06,680 --> 00:06:08,270
rid of its own meltwater.

199
00:06:08,420 --> 00:06:10,130
And there's more deliberate heat.

200
00:06:11,020 --> 00:06:12,800
Many fridges warm the edges of the cabinet

201
00:06:12,820 --> 00:06:14,940
where the doors seal, either with a loop

202
00:06:14,960 --> 00:06:17,460
of warm refrigerant tubing or with small

203
00:06:17,480 --> 00:06:18,770
electric heaters.

204
00:06:18,940 --> 00:06:21,140
The point is to stop condensation forming

205
00:06:21,160 --> 00:06:22,230
on the outside.

206
00:06:22,540 --> 00:06:24,520
Some models even have an energy saver

207
00:06:24,540 --> 00:06:26,790
switch that turns that heater off.

208
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:29,580
So the box that keeps your food cold has

209
00:06:29,600 --> 00:06:32,140
warm edges around its doors, and a heater

210
00:06:32,160 --> 00:06:34,790
mounted right next to its coldest coil.

211
00:06:35,600 --> 00:06:37,200
All of that shows up on your electricity

212
00:06:37,220 --> 00:06:37,970
bill.

213
00:06:38,120 --> 00:06:40,390
The good news is how small it has become.

214
00:06:40,600 --> 00:06:43,060
A new, efficient refrigerator uses a few

215
00:06:43,080 --> 00:06:44,850
hundred kilowatt hours a year.

216
00:06:45,220 --> 00:06:47,300
One eighteen cubic foot top freezer model

217
00:06:47,320 --> 00:06:49,700
on the ENERGY STAR list is rated at three

218
00:06:49,720 --> 00:06:51,680
hundred and sixty three kilowatt hours a

219
00:06:51,700 --> 00:06:52,250
year.

220
00:06:52,380 --> 00:06:54,360
That's about ten percent under the federal

221
00:06:54,380 --> 00:06:55,710
limit for its size.

222
00:06:56,100 --> 00:06:58,380
In nineteen seventy four, the average new

223
00:06:58,400 --> 00:07:00,910
fridge used more than eighteen hundred.

224
00:07:01,460 --> 00:07:03,680
New fridges use roughly a quarter of the

225
00:07:03,700 --> 00:07:05,880
energy of mid seventies models, and they

226
00:07:05,900 --> 00:07:06,750
hold more.

227
00:07:06,980 --> 00:07:08,740
Which brings us to the old advice about

228
00:07:08,760 --> 00:07:10,150
the open fridge door.

229
00:07:10,400 --> 00:07:12,570
Leaving it open won't cool your kitchen.

230
00:07:12,860 --> 00:07:14,520
Every bit of heat it pulls out goes

231
00:07:14,540 --> 00:07:16,340
straight back into the same room through

232
00:07:16,360 --> 00:07:18,560
the condenser, along with heat from the

233
00:07:18,580 --> 00:07:19,990
electricity it uses.

234
00:07:20,220 --> 00:07:21,970
So the room ends up warmer.

235
00:07:22,200 --> 00:07:24,980
All you get is a brief puff of cold air at

236
00:07:25,000 --> 00:07:25,690
the door.

237
00:07:26,460 --> 00:07:28,160
So your fridge doesn't make cold.

238
00:07:28,180 --> 00:07:29,340
It moves heat.

239
00:07:29,360 --> 00:07:31,880
Out of the cold box, around a loop of

240
00:07:31,900 --> 00:07:33,780
boiling and squeezing, and into your

241
00:07:33,800 --> 00:07:36,040
kitchen, with a little extra added on the

242
00:07:36,060 --> 00:07:36,630
way.

243
00:07:36,860 --> 00:07:38,440
Much of that warmth on the side of the

244
00:07:38,460 --> 00:07:41,020
cabinet is heat that came from inside your

245
00:07:41,040 --> 00:07:41,480
fridge.

246
00:07:41,500 --> 00:07:44,720
Next time on Designed Otherwise, another

247
00:07:44,740 --> 00:07:47,460
everyday machine that works in a way you'd

248
00:07:47,480 --> 00:07:49,190
never guess from the outside.
